Connect boundary displacement to pressure work and path dependence (@physics-boundary-work: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/balances/Boundary-work-quasistatic-paths-and-irreversibility.lingua#r_3FJYGFE1APRF36P7GC0QZYCW0P") { r_0C6366C3DAD812D2A1D81DFDC4
For an idealized piston face with area A moving a distance dx, a uniform opposing normal load p_ext A gives mechanical work against that load p_ext A dx=p_ext dV, because dV=A dx. Integrate the actual load/displacement relation for a finite change. To identify this load work with the gas’s boundary work, specify how piston inertia, friction and other mechanical storage are treated. In the quasistatic frictionless teaching paths below, they are negligible and the gas pressure at the face matches the applied opposing pressure in the limiting model. Then W_by=∫p dV along the equilibrium path.

An arbitrary rapid process need not have a single uniform system pressure at intermediate times. A curve through its initial/final equilibrium points is not automatically its work history. Likewise, slow motion can still involve friction or finite-temperature heat transfer; quasistatic and reversible are not synonyms. Read [Boundary work, quasistatic paths and irreversibility](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/balances/Boundary-work-quasistatic-paths-and-irreversibility.lingua#r_3FJYGFE1APRF36P7GC0QZYCW0P) for the existing general caution and later isothermal/free-expansion comparison. Its logarithmic formula is not needed for the algebraic paths here.

Let state A have V_A=0.002 m³ and p_A=150000 Pa, and state B have V_B=0.003 m³ and p_B=200000 Pa. These are stipulated equilibrium states of the fixed-amount ideal-gas model with nR=1 J/K, so T_A=300 K and T_B=600 K. They do not identify a real gas or validate a container. A path must specify intermediate states as well as these endpoints.

For the high-pressure path, first raise pressure at fixed V_A to p_B, then increase volume at p_B. The vertical step has dV=0; the horizontal step gives W_H=p_B(V_B−V_A)=200 J. For the low-pressure path, first increase volume at p_A, then raise pressure at fixed V_B, giving W_L=p_A(V_B−V_A)=150 J. Along a straight line in the p-V plane, integrate the affine pressure relation to get W_D=(p_A+p_B)(V_B−V_A)/2=175 J. The difference W_H−W_L=(p_B−p_A)(V_B−V_A)=50 J is the rectangle’s oriented area, not a difference of internal energy between A and B.

For U=(3/2)pV+constant, all three paths give ΔU=450 J. Their required heats are therefore 650, 600 and 625 J. The intermediate temperatures differ: the high-pressure corner is 400 K, while the low-pressure corner is 450 K. Heating arrangements would have to support the prescribed paths; the calculation does not establish a real process control method. Reversing a prescribed equilibrium path reverses the signed integrals. It does not establish that a particular dissipative experimental process can simply be reversed without other changes.

**Read and reconstruct:** [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), section 4.3, and [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), section 3.2, keeping their equilibrium-path conditions visible. Derive the diagonal work both from its linear pressure formula and from rectangle/triangle areas. Recover the high/low results segment by segment with units Pa·m³=J. An isolated free expansion of the same ideal gas from 0.002 to 0.003 m³ instead has zero external work and heat, unchanged model temperature 300 K and final pressure 100000 Pa. This is a different final state from B and cannot be assigned the equilibrium-path work above.

**Continue:** [Boundary work, quasistatic paths and irreversibility](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/balances/Boundary-work-quasistatic-paths-and-irreversibility.lingua#r_3FJYGFE1APRF36P7GC0QZYCW0P).

